But George Cohen convinced him and actually exemplified the principles of architectural honesty that Frank Lloyd Wright and other modernists championed. In many ways, George Cohen is the under-song hero of the Guggenheim, but Frank Lloyd Wright's respect for him is clear. Right by the front entrance, George Cohen's name is written in metal, embedded on the exterior wall, alongside a red square that serves as Frank Lloyd Wright's signature. This was the only time Wright ever put the general contractor's name on a building. From the next stop, head over to the cafe off the ramp on the third floor. Look out the window and you'll see the outer facade up close.
